  "content": {
    "id": "p57-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Factor numerator and denominator, and hence write down the zeroes and discontinuities.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p57-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2-5x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x(x-5)}$. Zeroes: none. Discontinuities: $x=0,5$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=\\frac{1}{x(x-5)}\\end{align*}$$The numerator is never zero, so there are no zeroes. The denominator is zero at $x=0$ and $x=5$, so these are the discontinuities.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      },
      {
        "id": "p57-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{x}{x^2-5x+6}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{x}{(x-2)(x-3)}$. Zero: $x=0$. Discontinuities: $x=2,3$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=\\frac{x}{(x-2)(x-3)}\\end{align*}$$The numerator is zero at $x=0$, where the denominator is nonzero. The denominator is zero at $x=2,3$, so these are the discontinuities.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 27
      },
      {
        "id": "p57-q5-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2-16}{x^2-9}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{(x-4)(x+4)}{(x-3)(x+3)}$. Zeroes: $x=\\pm4$. Discontinuities: $x=\\pm3$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=\\frac{(x-4)(x+4)}{(x-3)(x+3)}\\end{align*}$$The numerator is zero at $x=\\pm4$, where the denominator is nonzero. The denominator is zero at $x=\\pm3$, so these are the discontinuities.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 29
      }
    ]
  },
